Chapter: RealApproxModes
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The two combinations that could not be spelled before the Real collapse.
 Precision and safety are independent choices, and the type carried exactly
 one qualifier, so of the six combinations `Real approximate trapping` and
 `Real approximate saturating` were the two missing, and both are f32.

 THE WIDTH IS THE WHOLE RISK HERE. The saturating and trapping emitters
 test whether a result left the finite range by extracting the exponent and
 comparing it against all-ones, and both constants differ between the
 widths: a double is `shl 1, shr 53` against 2047, a single is `shl 33,
 shr 56` against 255. Run the f32 values through the f64 constants and the
 exponent read is nonsense, the guard never fires, and saturating quietly
 stops saturating. That is not hypothetical for this pair of functions --
 both modes already shipped once ignoring infinity entirely, and no test
 could see it because every operand was finite.

 So every case below is chosen to LEAVE the finite range, which is the only
 thing these modes are for.

Section: Operands

 Bit patterns rather than decimals, because the largest finite float and an
 infinity render alike and the boundary values have no short decimal form.
 `7F7FFFFF` is the largest finite single and `7F800000` is +inf, so the two
 differ by one in the bit pattern -- which is exactly what the saturating
 clamp exploits when it decrements.

  f32-max : Real approximate
  f32-max = bits-to-real-approx #7F7FFFFF

  f32-two : Real approximate
  f32-two = real-approx-from-int 2

Section: Saturating

  sat : Real approximate, Real approximate -> Real approximate saturating
  sat (a) (b) = to-real-approx-saturating a * to-real-approx-saturating b

  sat-add : Real approximate, Real approximate -> Real approximate saturating
  sat-add (a) (b) = to-real-approx-saturating a + to-real-approx-saturating b

  sat-bits : Real approximate saturating -> Integer
  sat-bits (x) = real-approx-to-bits (from-real-approx-saturating x)

Section: Report

 max * 2 overflows a single. Saturating must answer the largest finite
 single, `7F7FFFFF` = 2139095039, and NOT an infinity `7F800000` =
 2139095040. One apart, and the wrong one is what an unclamped result gives,
 so this line distinguishes a working clamp from an absent one by a single
 bit.

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("f32 max bits " & show (real-approx-to-bits f32-max))
    print-line-uni ("sat mul overflow " & show (sat-bits (sat f32-max f32-two)))
    print-line-uni ("sat add overflow " & show (sat-bits (sat-add f32-max f32-max)))
    print-line-uni ("sat finite stays " & show (sat-bits (sat f32-max (real-approx-from-int 0))))
  end
